  "name": "Question 18",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14093765/assets/daada2a7ad84b9f6db82706ad16da02a9b3cba329e56ced53d6b99b5852751ca.png)\n\nOne branch of a hyperbola (shown above) is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{tan}\u2061t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sec}\u2061t.\n$$\n\nDetermine the domain of the parameter $t$ for this branch, assuming that the entire hyperbola is defined only for $t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ].$\n\n$t\\in (${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$)$",
  "worked_solution": "The parametric equations of the full hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{tan}\u2061t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sec}\u2061t,\\,t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ],\\,t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a single branch, we refer to the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14093765/assets/212e8525a548d21774fec50b87ea34f368ac5821176b86859c14b1bfcef74407.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $t$ to $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ we get only the upper branch.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations that describe only the upper branch are\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{tan}\u2061t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sec}\u2061t,\\,t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).\n$$",
